    "title": {
      "en": "Trigonometric ratios of an acute angle and identities"
    },
    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "The flagpole"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "Standing 20 m from a flagpole you look up at its top at 45 degrees. How tall is it? Trigonometry links angles to lengths in a right triangle."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "sin, cos, tan",
          "cosec, sec, cot",
          "tan = sin/cos",
          "sin^2 A + cos^2 A = 1",
          "1 + tan^2 A = sec^2 A",
          "special angles",
          "complementary angles"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "In a right triangle, for an acute angle A: sin A = opposite/hypotenuse, cos A = adjacent/hypotenuse, tan A = opposite/adjacent.\nTheir reciprocals are cosec A = 1/sin A, sec A = 1/cos A, cot A = 1/tan A.\nAlso tan A = sin A / cos A.\nThe basic identity is sin squared A + cos squared A = 1.\nDividing it gives 1 + tan squared A = sec squared A and 1 + cot squared A = cosec squared A.\nValues of special angles: sin 30 = 1/2, sin 45 = 1/root 2, sin 60 = root 3 / 2, cos is the reverse, and tan 45 = 1.\nComplementary angles: sin (90 - A) = cos A and tan (90 - A) = cot A."
        }
      },
